  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t+1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\sqrt{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & =2\\sqrt{1+1}+C \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =2\\sqrt{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ C & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\sqrt{t+1}-\\sqrt{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
